00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:06.150 align:start position:0%
the first shipments of grain to avert a  looming global food crisis are expected  to leave ukraine this week despite the  war raging around its ports under united  nations brokered agreement with russia a  ukrainian minister says that shipments  of wheat corn and sunflower oil will  leave their black sea ports through the  newly negotiated safe channels within  days  the port in china mosque will be the  first one then odessa then port  overall in two weeks we'll be  technically ready to export out of all  the terminals of above mentioned ports  that is despite a russian strike on the  port of odessa on saturday russia's  foreign minister says there are no  barriers to the grain deliveries but  adds a warning yesterday  there's nothing in the obligation that  russia took including within the  framework of the agreement signed on  july 22nd in istanbul which prohibit us  from continuing the special military  operation destroying military  infrastructure and other military  targets  the saturday strike in odessa makes  clear that includes targets in the ports  it also includes ammunition depots the  russian military said on monday that it  struck one in ukraine's central kamal  nitsky region that housed u.s supplied  highmars rocket systems  ukraine's military says those precision  multi-launch rocket systems have changed  the balance in the war allowing them to  destroy 50 russian ammunition depots  since they arrived and ukraine is asking  for more to break through the russian  line  after all that our people have gone  through after all that we have learned  over the centuries ukrainians will never  give up their independence  both the russian and the ukrainian  strikes were reported in western ukraine  far from the front lines leaving no safe  haven in the country right now  as ukrainian forces push toward her son  in the south foreign fighters continue  to battle alongside ukrainian troops on  the eastern front near harkeef  for every every one of our boys that's  killed we have to avenge them  as the ports plan to resume grain  exports on the front lines the war of  attrition continues  john hendren al jazeera
 

